{"id": "pipeline.rtx5090_e2e", "statement": "The production-mode real-scene pipeline runs end to end in {value} on the RTX 5090", "value": "6.92 s", "kind": "measurement", "status": "current", "as_of": {"date": "2026-08-26", "version": "0.2.1"}, "source": {"doc": "SDK.md#02-findings-running-log (5090 productionization)", "generator": "gpugate reports 20260826-b978b2 \u2014 deleted upstream 2026-08-29; archived at docs/reports/20260826-b978b2.md"}, "patterns": ["\\b(6\\.92\\s*s)\\b"], "cites": ["SDK.md"], "lane": "facts", "notes": "PROVENANCE RECOVERED and now in-repo. The generating report was deleted upstream (HTTP 404 from 2026-08-29, while its same-day sibling 20260826-ed92e7 still resolved), and the file previously cited as evidence, gpubench/bench-5090.json, described a different synthetic workload and never held an end-to-end figure. The report text was recovered from a local session transcript, verified byte-identical against a surviving scratch copy, and committed. It derives the figure in full: 118 s validate -> 13.1 s production mode -> 8.25 s binned decode_slice -> 6.92 s fused cell_decode, with a stage breakdown of encode 2.78s, slices 0.70s, mip encode 1.83s, x-ray 0.29s. SINGLE-RUN TIMING ON A SHARED BOX - treat as one observation, not a reproducible figure. Five holo-bench runs on 2026-08-28 gave bit-identical checksums (the numerics are genuinely deterministic) but readout_s split 0.1705/0.1707 against 0.3606/0.3675/0.3747, a 2.1x swing inside one sitting.", "evidence": ["docs/reports/20260826-b978b2.md"]}
